[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — broken stdout pipe: exit 0 instead of death by SIGPIPE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Severity: real, and invisible to any stdout-only test.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs. A C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program inherits the default disposition. So when stdout is a pipe whose reader
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m has gone away:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | exit status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | exited 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduced by creating a pipe, closing the read end, and passing the write end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m as the child's stdout, so the very first write hits `EPIPE`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/build/driver:                  returncode=-13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation/target/release/driver:   returncode=0     <- mismatch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Rust runtime start-up code masks `SIGPIPE`; the previous translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m additionally swallowed the write error with `let _ = writeln!(...)`, so nothing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was left to observe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Restore the default disposition at the top of `main`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal` is declared in a small `extern "C"` block rather than by adding the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `libc` crate, since Rust already links the C runtime on Linux.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Guarded by** `broken_stdout_pipe_kills_both_with_sigpipe`. Verified to be a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m live test by negative control: deleting the `signal` call makes it fail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`broken-pipe mismatch`), and restoring it makes it pass.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — stdout buffering discipline did not match glibc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Severity: not observable through stdout/stderr/exit status for this program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Corrected anyway for faithfulness; see the honest caveat below.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc chooses the buffering mode for `stdout` from what fd 1 is:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * a TTY -> line buffered, one write per `\n`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * anything else (pipe, file, closed fd) -> fully buffered, so this program's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whole 74-byte output leaves in a single `write` at exit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust's `io::stdout()` is a `LineWriter` unconditionally, so the previous
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation issued one write per line even when stdout was a pipe. Combined with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the fix for mismatch 1, that difference is what decides who gets killed when a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reader stops reading partway through: the C program has already handed over all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 74 bytes in one successful write, whereas a line-buffered writer can be killed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m between lines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `CStdout` in `src/main.rs` emulates the glibc discipline: it picks the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mode from `isatty(1)` once, accumulates into a `Vec`, flushes on `\n` only in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m line-buffered mode, flushes on a full buffer (`BUFSIZ`, 4096), and flushes at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit the way stdio does when `main` returns.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Honest caveat, established by negative control.** Forcing the translation back
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to line buffering (`let line_buffered = true;`) leaves the *entire* suite green.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For this program the buffering mode is genuinely unobservable through the three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m graded channels, because:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the output is 74 bytes, far below the 64 KiB pipe capacity, so no write ever
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   blocks or is split;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the program finishes in microseconds, so a reader cannot realistically close
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   between two writes;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * on `/dev/full` and on a closed fd 1, every write fails either way and C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ignores all the return values, so the status is 0 regardless;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * on a TTY the terminal applies ONLCR to the same bytes in either mode.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So `reader_closing_early_matches` does **not** prove what an earlier version of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m its comment claimed; the comment was corrected rather than left overstating the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m evidence. The emulation is kept because it is what glibc does, and it removes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the divergence in write granularity that only a syscall trace would expose.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
